# The command recipe must survive a reused model id, run inside real ChimeraX.
#
#   ChimeraX --nogui --silent --exit --script tests/integration/recipe_after_reopen.cxc
#
# ChimeraX reuses model ids: close #1 and open something else and the new
# structure is #1 too. Both of the recipe's defences key on that fact, and both
# had a hole in them.
#
#   - `_record` deduped on the command text alone, so the reopened structure's
#     characterise line read identically to the closed structure's and was
#     suppressed as a repeat. `_live_entries` then dropped the surviving line
#     as dead, and the report came out claiming a complete recipe with nothing
#     in it — which is self-refuting, and reproducibility is the manuscript's
#     central claim.
#   - `_live_entries` tested membership by `id()`, and CPython reuses
#     addresses, so the opposite failure was available too: a closed
#     structure's line matching whatever now sits at that address.
#
# The unit tests cover both in isolation and passed throughout, including while
# the live sequence was failing. That is the reason this file exists: the parts
# were each correct and the sequence was not, so only the sequence can prove it.
#
# Expected: the report lists exactly one command, the characterise issued after
# the reopen, and its provenance sentence is therefore true.

close session
open 1brs

# First structure, fully analysed, then closed. Its recipe line must not
# survive into the report below.
molcompose characterise A D model #1
close #1

# Second structure. ChimeraX hands it the same id, and its characterise line
# is byte-identical to the one just discarded.
open 1brs
molcompose characterise A D model #1

# One command, from the live structure. Zero means the dedupe swallowed it;
# two means a closed structure's line came back.
molcompose report /tmp/molcompose-recipe-after-reopen.json model #1

close session
